00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.160
In the Season Two finale when Logan
tells Kendall he’s making his son

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.840
the “blood sacrifice” for the cruise
scandal plaguing Waystar Royco,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.920
this scene contains three key moments
of dialogue that basically tell us

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.520
everything about who Logan is, as
a businessman and as a father.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.160
One involves the Roy family
belief that, in order to

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.520
make it in business, you
have to have killer instinct.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.200
“Did you ever think I could do it?”

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.400
“The top job?”

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.456
“You’re not a killer.
You have to be a killer.”

00:02:01.456 --> 00:02:03.920
Rewatching Seasons One and Two,
we’ll actually notice that

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.200
we were told all along that
Kendall’s lack of killer instinct

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.920
was why his dad doubted him—-
from the first episode when

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.680
Logan tells Kendall he “bent” for the
Vice-like media company named Vaulter.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.600
“I hear you let the
guy from the website

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.760
trash talk you, and
you just let him come.”

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.280
“I hear it played weak”

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.040
to Shiv’s line in the second episode

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.240
“You lack killer instinct”

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.400
“I don’t think all that, I’m
just trying to be dad’s voice.”

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.440
to the two plots in Season One
when Logan berates his son

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.280
for coming for him and losing

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.080
“You lost, son.”

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.880
Logan’s view of business
is that it’s a battle.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.080
“What the f[BLEEP]’s going’s on?

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.720
He’s selling me things I
want at a fair price?”

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.440
“He’s smiling.”

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.800
“It’s no good if he’s smiling.”

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.200
When Roman half-heartedly
tries to suggest that there’s

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.720
such a thing as a win-win,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.160
“Objectively, I mean, if we get what
we want and he gets what he wants”

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.800
Logan counters that there’s no
success without hurt and killing:

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.600
“You screw them out and
then you watch them squeal.”
 &nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.080
“I like hurting human beings
as much as the next guy.”

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.280
The very first episode of Succession
is entitled “celebration”

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.440
--as the family plans to celebrate
both tycoon Logan Roy’s birthday

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.080
and the planned announcement
that Kendall will replace

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.440
his father as CEO--

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.280
but really the whole birthday party
is a test, one which Kendall fails

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.280
by choosing to put family first.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.880
“You left the room. The deal.”

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.960
“To come to my dad’s
f[BLEEP]ing birthday party!”

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.480
After Logan announces that he’s not
going to pass power on to Kendall

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.720
after all, he reveals that he
thinks Kendall is too soft to be CEO.
 &nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.160
“Sometimes, it IS a
big dick competition.”

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.120
Kendall comes to understand that,
if he really wants the top job,

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.160
he’ll have to kill his father
(in a business sense).

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.160
“The only way he’ll respect you
is if you try to destroy him,

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.320
because in your position that is
exactly what he would try to do.”

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.160
“I can’t do that.”

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.800
“Well obviously,
so have fun in Shanghai.”

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.520
But whenever Kendall mounts an
attempt on his father’s business life,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.440
he self-sabotages at the last-minute,
seemingly proving his father right

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.080
that he just can’t
summon the will to kill.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.720
“Only 15 percent of men
when it comes to it

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.160
actually shoot an
undefended enemy soldier.”

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.280
Mid-way through the season, Kendall
plots a vote of no confidence,

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.840
but makes the incredibly
bad decision to fly out to

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.360
Long Island to convince Ilona,
(a vote he actually doesn’t need),

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.560
thus missing the vote and letting
his father muscle him out of the win

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.920
by refusing to leave the room
and bullying yes’s into reversing

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.680
their vote or abstaining.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.480
At the end of Season One,
Kendall decides again to

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.040
kill his father--this time
through a hostile takeover

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.200
with his private equity buddy Stewie
and his father’s enemy Sandy Furness.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.358
“It’s good to demonize
the enemy before a fight—”

00:04:27.358 --> 00:04:27.920
“Yeah, yeah, yeah.”

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.320
“It’s good to demonize him
before you have to kill his ass.”

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.840
In the Season One finale, we hear
a callback to the first episode.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.720
“It’s a celebf[BLEEP]ingbration--
you did it man.”

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.680
But again, just as Kendall’s
on the verge of actually getting

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.720
what he wants, he panics and
tells Frank about the plan

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.560
(even though Stewie
warned him not to),

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.080
thus spurring a leak which means
the bear hug has to be accelerated

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.760
and Kendall has to confront his
dad face to face at the wedding.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.200
“you’re very tough and so am I..
I’m-I’m not sorry for what I’m doing,

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.120
but I’m sorry for how
it makes you feel.”

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.280
Kendall spirals out,
going on a bender,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.880
“Yo, uh, do you have any more…”
“We got a huge day tomorrow.”

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.240
driving intoxicated, and causing
the death of a young caterer.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.680
So at this point, rather than killing
his father, it seems that what Kendall

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.040
really wants is to revert to being
that helpless dependent child,

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.160
to be “rescued” by his
powerful dad’s safe arms.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.640
“You’re my boy. You’re
my number 1 boy.”

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.000
“I’m sorry.”
“No no no.”

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.880
Season Two follows “broken” Kendall
who’s so determined to serve his dad

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.120
loyally that he’s
behaving like a robot,

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.400
“Look at you scanning for
influence like a yuppie robocop.”

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.640
who’d consider even a
thought independent of

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.080
his father as disobedient.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.720
“You have a hard time finding
a happy medium between

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.560
worshipping him and
wanting to kill him.”
 &nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.080
But while at first doing exactly
what his dad wants does seem to bring

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.120
the two closer together than ever,
it ultimately leads to his father

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.440
exploiting him at the
expense of Kendall’s health,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.280
and planning to sacrifice him.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.480
So all this explains why, in
that final moment of Season Two--

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.080
as a father, Logan is happy that
Kendall revealed he is a killer

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.200
. . . something Roman and Shiv
have not yet proved themselves to be.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.680
“You better be smelling your
f[BLEEP]ing armpit, Romulus.”

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.640
“It's the sort of tough choice
people need to be able to make.”

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.920
“I can’t choose, dad”
“No.”

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.520
Of course, Logan the CEO and
business titan is still gonna

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.080
fight tooth and nail against
his son in Season Three,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.640
but this is the look of a father
who is proud of his son

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.640
for proving a worthy adversary.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.240
A second key moment in the father-son
scene is where Kendall reveals why

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.800
he’s willing to accept
this public punishment.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.400
“I deserve it.”
“Oh no son--”

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.800
“Yeah. For everything.”
“No, no, no, God no.”

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.200
“The boy.”
“Nah nah nah Not that.”
&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.160
His father makes it clear he
literally doesn’t care about

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.560
or believe in any of that moralizing.
But what Logan has done to his son,

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.800
which is both genius and evil,
is to weaponize the boy’s better self--

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.320
the human being who feels and
cares about things--against him

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.760
“Are you gonna f[BLEEP]ing cry?”

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.640
to make Kendall view that self
(and his emotions in general)

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.520
as weakness, and to use
it to control Kendall.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.040
“Keep clean this weekend, eh killer.”

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.440
It’s actually this nice,
moral person in Kendall,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.080
who wants to be a “good son”
and do the right thing,

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.960
who gets in his way whenever
he’s about to kill his father.
&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:20.720
“Is this, like, objectively horrible?”

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.640
“You might never get another shot…
Strength. You’re a good son.”
 &nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.680
It’s right after this line
that Kendall self-sabotages

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.640
over the vote of no confidence,
making the call to Ilona’s family

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.040
which Frank warns him
will stir things up--

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:36.000
because on some level he
wants to mess up his plan

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.920
so he’ll still be a “good son.”

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.920
When Logan reassures his son that
the boy Kendall killed “doesn’t matter”

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.400
he uses a key phrase that
we’ve heard pop up in the

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.280
investigation of the cruises scandal. 
 
“NRPI. No real person involved.”
 &nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.800
Logan’s casual use of the phrase is
important because it confirms that,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.440
if we had any doubts, Waystar Royco’s
toxic culture really does totally

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:58.640
stem from him.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.880
As Senator Gil Eavis puts it,
“Emerson said, ‘Every institution

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.640
is the shadow of a single man.’
At Waystar, that man is Logan Roy,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.040
isn't he?”

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.480
not to mention that--as Kendall notes--
Logan is clearly aware of everything

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.360
that goes on:

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.760
“My father keeps a watchful eye…
and the notion he would allow millions

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:23.040
of dollars to be paid without his
explicit approval is utterly fanciful.”

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.440
So Logan is the head that
needs to be cut off.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.720
This is why the first thing Kendall
says about his dad in the press

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.520
conference is that Logan
is a “malignant presence.”

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.200
“The truth is that my father
is a malignant presence,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.080
a bully and a liar.”

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.160
In other words, the energy of his very
being and the behavior he’s trained

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.920
others to view as normal create
an “evil” company culture

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:48.000
from which all
transgressions originate.
&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.080
Waystar Royco’s toxic culture is
encapsulated in this phrase “NRPI”:

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.440
this idea that some people (even
most people) don’t matter:

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.160
“NRPI.”
“No Real Person Involved"

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:05.680
"Uh-huh and what does that mean?"

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.360
"That means it's a sex worker or
a migrant worker at a foreign port."

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.040
The series underlines that the way
this abusive power structure functions

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.280
(in both Waystar and within the family)
is by encouraging each individual to

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.280
pass down the pain they get from their
higher-ups onto those beneath them.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.400
“You pour the shit I’m pouring
on you on your f[BLEEP]ing minions

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:27.200
and you ride them.”

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.120
“You know what? Put the
f[BLEEP]ing snacks away!”
 &nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.160
This cultural expectation that
you take the shit you get out

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.680
on the people below you is
shown most clearly through Greg,

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.680
the newcomer and distant relative who’s
the lowest on the family totem pole,

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.800
and so is used as a
punching bag by everyone--

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.560
sometimes quite literally.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:45.840
“Ow! Dude!”

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.160
“I might punch-bag you
a little, is that cool?”

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.560
Mostly, Greg is there
to take shit from Tom.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.040
“The thing about me is I’m
a terrible terrible prick.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:57.360
I got you again!
I just got you!”

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.800
who--as Shiv’s partner and a
relative “nobody” in the Roys’ view

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.280
--is that one level up from Greg
and needs someone who’s less important

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.320
but still in the “family” to
let out his negative feelings on.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.520
“Just thought family would be safest,
and Greg's expendable. You know.”

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.240
“What, you-you'd kill Greg?”

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.040
“No. But you know, he's family,
right? So we shut him up.”
 &nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.320
As a born Roy, Roman is a
level up from Tom, but he’s also

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.600
the baby of the family who’s known
as a screw-up, so still low

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.360
on the family ladder--thus we
see him mistreat both Tom and Greg.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:31.120
“Dude, come on come on, stop.”

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:32.880
“They don’t have paper towels,
they got all green and shit.”

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.080
“Dude, seriously!”

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.640
Shiv actually takes out
her bullying instincts on

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.560
her fiance slash husband Tom.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.200
“I mean the wedding might be
in England but it might not.”

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.800
“Okay okay sorry sorry.”

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.920
And because Tom can’t honestly
voice or address his powerlessness

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.040
in his relationship with Shiv,
he literally beats Greg up

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.760
when Greg tries to be honest
about seeing Shiv cheating.
&nbsp;

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.400
“Shut the f[BLEEP] up, Greg.”
“I’m trying to help you.”

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.240
“Shut up. I told you to
f[BLEEP]ing shut up.”

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.040
He also violently throws water
bottles at the kid when Greg

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.280
tries to get out of working at the Fox
News-like ATN by uttering the sentence

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.400
“It-it could be like, like a
business open relationship.”

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.280
which strikes a nerve because
it echoes the open relationship

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.280
Tom doesn’t want with his wife.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:14.960
All this bullying underlines
how power imbalances corrupt

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.160
any relationship.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.200
And of course, this culture
of exploiting and punishing

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.560
the weaknesses of the people you
supposedly love and are family with,

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.480
originates with Logan himself.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:25.760
When Greg tells Logan

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.120
“I don’t want to mention any
names because I *stammers*

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:32.800
but the culture there, it borders
on the personally abusive at times.”

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:33.760
“Tom?”

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.640
Logan actually laughs and
seems almost to approve--

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:38.400
to be proud or impressed by&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.280
Tom’s surprising cruelty.
 
“I didn’t know he had it in him.”

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.720
just as Tom, too, is proud
when Greg shows he’s learning

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.120
“slime ball” killer-instinct tactics.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.680
“Are you asking if
you can blackmail me?”

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.680
*laughs*
“You’re a f[BLEEP]ing slime ball!”  
 &nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.320
What Logan relishes most of all
is something that he and the

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:59.440
family vaguely refer to,
in a variety of contexts,

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:00.400
as “the game”

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:03.280
“It’s all part of the game.”
“Oh it’s all part of the game!

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.760
“Come on everybody. It’s a rootin
tootin super f[BLEEP]ing fun game

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:10.400
for all the family,
step right up!”

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:13.680
In the first episode, we’re introduced
to this through a literal ballgame

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.200
Logan is itching to play

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.160
“I think it’s time to play the game…”

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.040
but even the baseball game
isn’t really about that game--

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:24.000
it’s an opportunity for him to
work each of his children in

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.720
business negotiations around
changing the family trust.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.480
“Okay let’s play ball.”

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.240
likewise, we’ll see in later episodes,
often the play is blinding others to

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.920
where the real game is and which
big moves are happening outside of

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:36.960
the apparent game.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.520
But strikingly, all
of the constant “games”

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:41.440
we witness the Roy circle playing

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:42.720
“Is there an angle here?”

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.240
“I’m not the f[BLEEP]ing angle.”

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.080
are not fun at all.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.560
“Tom! sit on the floor. It's fun.”
“Seriously?”

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.264
“Yeah it’s a game. Boar on the floor.”
“Why am I in this?”

00:12:53.264 --> 00:12:55.200
“How the f[BLEEP] would I know Greg,
you think I have a rational

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:56.000
explanation for this?”

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.120
--they’re based on humiliation…
on someone being the winner who

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:02.400
inflicts cruelty and
public shame on a loser

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:05.520
“The rules are that you’re
spared if you tell the truth--”

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:08.080
“There are rules?
Do you know something Greg?

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:12.080
There are no f[BLEEP]ing rules.”

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.280
And all this circles back
again to that NRPI acronym,

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:19.040
which expresses that human beings
matter only insofar as they are

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.200
reflections of wealth and power.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.600
“Mia’s a real person, with a face.”

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.880
“The game” really stands in for
capitalism and corporate power

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.520
in today’s world--
and what Succession is underlining,

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.880
is that this “game” is
based on a refusal to value

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.400
the humanity of people.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.520
Because it’s only by blinding oneself
to the fact that talking about,

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:39.840
for example, waving
“skulls” to shareholders,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.320
means actual people
losing their livelihoods.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:45.840
“In terms of skulls, how
many skulls do you think?”

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.115
“That we can lose?”
“Yeah.”

00:13:48.115 --> 00:13:50.880
“I don’t know. Like, I
guess like thirty? Or fifty?”

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.160
“Fifty skulls?”

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.160
and ignoring the fact that this game
is no good and no fun for any of us;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.200
it actually makes even those
at the top pretty miserable.
 &nbsp;

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.160
“He’s pretty lonely,
and-and afraid I guess.”

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:05.200
Logan passes down his hurt,
especially onto his

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:06.640
primary son Kendall.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.040
During his visit back to
the UK to be honored in

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.160
his hometown of Dundee, Scotland,
Logan’s trying to push away

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.160
difficult feelings
about his own aging.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.760
And after Kendall makes the
mistake of being honest about Rhea,

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:22.640
“It's just maybe not a good look.
The old f-not-not the old fool…”

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:26.000
“That's how she could make you seem…”
Just-Just being honest.”

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:30.160
Logan responds by inflicting a
kind of emotional torture on his son

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:33.600
by forcing Kendall to visit
the family of the boy he killed.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.600
“Will you come with me?”
“As in?”

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.240
“Down to the boy’s family.”

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.720
We also see the way this
“pass-down-the-pain” culture

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.880
extends to how Logan’s children treat
common people outside their circle.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.840
In the first episode, Roman thinks
it’s funny to play with a young kid’s

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.160
hopes and dreams by offering
him a million bucks if he

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.640
can hit a home run.
 
“Rom, don’t be an asshole.”

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.720
“It’s a game, jeez,
stop being so serious.”

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.400
passing on the public humiliation
he’s experienced so often due to his

00:14:58.400 --> 00:14:59.920
father and brother’s “games.”

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:03.600
“You were so damn close,
oh that is so sad!”

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.520
“So take this back to your life.”

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.880
This kind of behavior is taken to
an extreme in the Season One finale,

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.480
during which the central Roy children
callously destroy other people:

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.600
in addition to Kendall killing
someone, Roman pushes up a launch

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:19.920
that’s not ready, causing an explosion,
and Shiv hurts her groom by revealing

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.720
her affair and “adult”
view of their relationship.
&nbsp;

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.840
“You know because I thought
we were both grown-ups

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:27.600
and we had an agreement.”
“Did we?”

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:31.520
None of them cause hurt intentionally,
but through a thoughtlessness

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:34.160
that stems from how they’ve
been taught to see themselves

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.360
as the only “real” people,
at the center of the universe,

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:39.200
different and better
than everyone else…

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.880
and blind to the need to act
responsibly toward other humans.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:51.680
A third key moment in the
Season Two Finale father-son scene

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:55.520
is when Logan justifies his choice
to “kill” Kendal by citing

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:58.640
the Incas’ rationale for
sacrificing children:

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:02.560
“What could you possibly
kill that you love so much

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.240
it would make the sun rise again?”

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:09.920
This is exactly the kind of
supernatural trick Logan is

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:12.800
reaching for--making the sun
rise again for himself,

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.960
i.e., giving himself more life.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.360
Succession has a lot
of Oedipus references.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:19.200
“You &amp; Kendall are thinking
of killing your dad?

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.160
That’s a little Greek tragedy.”
“Yeah, Oedipus.”

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:22.560
“Well--”

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:23.200
--a lot.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.880
“And you must be Oedipus Roy.”

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:26.800
“You should probably go and
poke your eyes out now.”

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:29.600
--many of which focus on Kendall
wanting to kill his father

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:31.520
and Roman wanting to
f*** his mother

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.080
(which we’ll have more time
to get into in another video!)

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:35.600
“I screwed mom for you.”

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:37.120
“You may want to
screw your mother--”

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.440
“Did I say that I was
going to f[BLEEP] Marcia? No.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.520
Although I definitely
would cause she’s hot but that’s--

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.000
You know. That’s like phase two.”

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.840
But in addition to
the Oedipal idea that

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.360
the child must destroy the parent,
the father-son finale scene underlines

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.400
that Logan is a “dark father,”
in the vein of Darth Vadar or

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:56.800
Greek mythology’s Kronos--
the figure who would

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.880
steal life from his own child.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:01.760
Succession evokes Shakespeare’s
King Lear through its premise

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:05.680
(and its star Brian Cox, who says
that he’s played Lear over “150 times”)

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:07.600
but it’s kind of an inverse Lear.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:09.920
While that play deals with
Lear’s folly in giving away

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.720
his kingdom to his children
before he’s actually dead,

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.640
here, as Cox points out, it’s the
opposite: “he’s taking it back”

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.520
Over time, it becomes clear
that Logan’s resisting the

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:22.560
succession choice, not just
because he’s keenly aware that

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.920
all the candidates are lacking,
but also because he’s resisting

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:26.960
his own death.

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:31.040
“Shoutout to Rhea for planning a
wonderful funeral. I mean memorial.”

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:36.880
“It’s my honor to salute a man who
will one day bury us all, no doubt.”

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:40.320
When Kendall talks Naomi Pierce into
considering the deal for the Roys

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.560
to acquire the Pierce
family’s news business,

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:45.760
he sells her on the idea
that money is freedom.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.400
“You can take the money,
then you’re free.”

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:50.640
But for the Roy children,
it’s the opposite:

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.200
their father’s money has trapped
them into what’s effectively

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:57.040
a very small existence--where their
whole world is about striving for

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:00.320
their father’s approval
(and by extension, love)

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.560
while knowing deep down
they’ll never get it.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.240
“I've managed to get myself
into this situation where

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:06.960
‘What does my dad think?’
is my entire f[BLEEP]ing universe.”

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.200
They’re frequently talking
about being “adults”

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.160
“We’re both adults.”

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:12.320
“The adults are talking, how about
you go play in the other room?”

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:15.600
because they all feel like little kids
who are constantly worrying about

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:16.720
what their dad thinks.

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:20.720
“Has anyone ever told you that you
talk about your dad, like, a lot?”

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.800
As ﻿Dr. Patti Britton told The Cut,
everyone in the show besides Logan,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.880
“lives in a kind of
caricature of themselves,

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:30.320
and a projection of what they believe
their father expects of them.” 
 &nbsp;

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:33.120
“No I don’t want to call my dad,
do you want to call your dad?

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:34.560
Does anyone want
to call their dad?

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.080
“Yeah, I’m right in
the middle, Dad.”

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:44.240
The Roys have internalized the
idea from their father that being

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:48.640
rich, powerful, and successful
requires you to be a cruel, heartless,

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.160
overall bad person who’s
totally shut off to feelings.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:54.480
One of the running themes
of the series is the way that

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:57.360
Logan uses rhetoric of caring
about his family when it

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:58.800
suits his business goals.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.800
“We’ve always pushed
that you’re a good dad.”

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:01.600
“Because?”

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:02.960
“Well because you
are a good dad.”

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:06.960
“There is nothing more
important than family.”

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.240
“He's joking, right?”

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.960
but shows time and again
that his children’s well-being

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:14.880
always comes second
to the company.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:18.400
“This is a family business.
But the family is fucked.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:19.920
And it’s hurting the stock.”

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.000
Logan has no time for discussing
his or anyone else’s feelings.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:28.080
“I guess what I want is for you
to tell us how you are feeling.”
 &nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.920
“I feel that I need
to check my emails.”

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:33.760
“That’s not a feeling dad.”

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:36.000
and moreover, he views
them as a weakness--

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.480
one which he can exploit
in order to get an edge

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:40.480
in business and
manipulate his kids.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:43.840
When Logan stages a family
therapy session at Connor’s retreat

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:46.640
for a photoshoot and to
revamp the company’s image,

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:50.160
the episode (like the ranch) is
named after a Napoleonic battle;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.160
the therapist ends
up in the hospital,

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.720
“He uh smashed his
teeth out on the pool.”

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:57.040
“Oh my god.”
“Freud would have had a field day.”

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.480
and the actual sharing of feelings
later that night is an all-out

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.360
emotional brawl with the
father bullying the kids.

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:06.640
“You’re marrying a man fathoms
beneath you because you don’t want

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:08.640
to risk being betrayed.”
&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:11.440
Kendall’s, Shiv’s and Roman’s
English mother is likewise

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.920
pathologically averse to
sharing feelings or

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.600
having them shared with her.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:16.560
“So how are you?”

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:20.800
“Well I might just as well ask you
if you’re going to go into all that.”

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.840
This is most poignantly illustrated
when Kendall attempts to tell her

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.720
about his emotional struggles
over the death of the boy he killed,

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:31.920
and his mother rejects his
request for a heart-to-heart:

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:36.880
“Are they difficult things? Because…
bit tired for home truths.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:38.960
it might be better to
do it over an egg.”

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:42.800
then purposely misses breakfast just
to avoid hearing Kendall’s feelings.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:44.720
“She’s sorry if she
misses our departure,

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.840
she’s got quite a lot to do.”

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.160
Even her bargaining to
have them come for Christmas

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:52.800
is just another game to
prove to them that their dad

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:54.720
will choose real estate over them.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:58.480
“He can keep the Summer Palace
if I get the family every Christmas.

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:00.160
Let’s make him choose.”

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.800
Each of the first two seasons
has a moment when Logan is

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:04.400
actually physically abusive--

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:08.240
and in both cases the showy reaction
that it’s not okay feels like

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:10.720
a jarring form of
protesting too much,

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.680
because (whether or not he’s
been physically abusive much before,

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:14.880
which isn’t clear)

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:21.120
“Did I even make contact? Because
that’s not something I do.”

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.320
Logan is constantly emotionally
abusive and bullying.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:25.760
“Everybody thinks you’re a joke.”

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:29.280
He only gives his kids love and
approval when they’re totally broken

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:30.400
and in need of him.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.920
If they start to appear too happy,
independent or flying high,

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:37.840
“But she’s kind of a
good one for me, Dad.”

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.120
he cuts their wings.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:42.240
So by infantilizing them,
shaming them, keeping them as

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:46.720
helpless dependent children,
he stays as the top dog important guy

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:47.280
--forever--

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:49.360
stealing back time
from his own kids.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:52.720
“He loves me he-he-he does.
I think it’s just a wrong

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:54.160
kind of love expression.”

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:59.120
“Yeah. He loves the broken you.
That’s what he loves.”

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:02.160
All of the kids have an internal
split between two selves--

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:04.640
a “nice” or “real”
person who feels things,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:08.080
and a “hot shit,” “exciting bastard”
persona they perform because

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:11.440
they think it’s what will prove to
their dad they’re good at “the game.”

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:13.760
They attempt to succeed
by being dead inside,

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:17.040
but then inevitably their feelings
rise up and get in the way.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.840
The kids are all surprised and
taken off guard whenever

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.920
one offers the other
genuine emotion:

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:26.080
“Come here, give me a hug. Come here.”
“A hug?”

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:29.840
They’re also intensely full of shame,
and trying very hard to hide

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:32.560
how desperately they crave
for their dad to acknowledge

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.880
that they’re smart and
can do the top job,

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:37.280
when it’s clearly written
on their faces the second

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:39.360
they get a drop of validation.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:43.280
“Logan, do you realize how much
power you wield in this room?”

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.240
“What do you mean?”

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:45.920
“No, he has a lot of
power everywhere.”

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:49.440
As ﻿Dr. Tammy Nelson, sex therapist
and author told the Cut:

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:52.320
“In many ways shame is the
flip side of narcissism…

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.120
the show is about… this
incredible narcissism of the father,

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.560
and how he has created these
narcissistic extensions of himself.

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.440
It creates kids that are
these little balls of shame.”

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:03.280
“You know, my guy says
that if dad had had therapy,

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:04.560
I wouldn’t need so much.”

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:06.080
“My guy’s surprised I
got through it at all.”

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:07.840
“Not sure you did.”

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:10.400
What all the kids need is
to find a way to reconcile

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:13.760
these two sides of themselves--
the narcissist and the ashamed person,

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:15.600
the “hot shit” and
the “nice” person--

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.440
to stop believing that strength
and feelings are inherently at odds.

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:22.880
Ironically, as Kendall realizes
at last in the Season Two Finale,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:26.640
in his case, “killing his father”
is the “right” and moral thing to do.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.640
“And it’s the right thing to do.”

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:31.680
--so in fact, his moral
feelings can be his power.

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:34.000
It’s this journey toward
being a real person--

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.800
who feels things as part of being
a mature adult in the world--

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:40.000
that increasingly looks like
the Roys’ way out of the hell

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.600
